s the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "At $t=0,$ the supplied model gives $Q(0)=\\alpha(1-0/50)+\\beta(1-0/50)^{3}=\\alpha+\\beta.$ The initial amount is $13\\,\\text{kg},$ so $\\alpha+\\beta=13.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540435 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a9cb43b-d28f-5ebe-81f2-e5f06d53503e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540400} {:answer/id #uuid "5de82b49-8912-5fcf-ab8d-93e04cb8d4d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540408} {:answer/id #uuid "5f5ebcbc-8c69-5cc9-bf5c-823ff2e14600" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540410} {:answer/id #uuid "6a6d2b86-3505-5e3f-8791-342345d726c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540413} {:answer/id #uuid "aa91f25f-da97-5d4c-83a0-9983d280ff08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "86" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa91f25f-da97-5d4c-83a0-9983d280ff08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "86" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540430} :answer-field/id #uuid "f42e40e6-0759-5913-8d45-3a2bc6a1081c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c92cf723-bd03-5192-8480-80c9e6d48364" :question/problem "A fruit syrup tank initially holds $1200\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $10\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved sweetener.\nA solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved sweetener is pumped into the tank at a rate of $36\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $48\\,\\text{L/min}.$ If the amount of sweetener $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n\n$$\nQ(t)=\\alpha \\,(1-\\frac{t}{100})+\\beta {(1-\\frac{t}{100})}^{4}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants, what is the value of $\\alpha -\\beta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of dissolved substance in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. The amount entering minus the amount leaving gives\n$$\\frac{dQ}{dt}=Q'_{\\text{in}}-Q'_{\\text{out}}.$$\nThe units of these rates are $\\text{kg/min}.$\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which substance enters the tank.\n$$\\begin{aligned}Q'_{\\text{in}}&=(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot(36\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\\\\n&=(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot(36\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\\\&=1.44\\,\\text{kg/min}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which substance exits the tank.\nThe volume is\n$$V(t)=1200+36t-48t=(1200-12t)\\,\\text{L}.$$\nThe concentration is $\\frac{Q}{1200-12t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.$ Hence,\n$$\\begin{aligned}Q'_{\\text{out}}&=(48\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot(\\frac{Q}{1200-12t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\\\\n&=(48\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot(\\frac{Q}{1200-12t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\\\\n&=\\frac{48Q}{1200-12t}\\,\\text{kg/min}=\\frac{4Q}{100-t}\\,\\text{kg/min}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}&=Q'_{\\text{in}}-Q'_{\\text{out}}\\\\&=1.44-\\frac{4Q}{100-t}.\\end{aligned}$$\nThus,\n$$\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{100-t}Q=1.44.$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n$$\\begin{aligned}I(t)&=e^{\\int 4/(100-t)\\,dt}\\\\&=e^{-4\\ln|100-t|}\\\\&=e^{\\ln (100-t)^{-4}}\\\\&=(100-t)^{-4}.\\end{aligned}$$\nMultiplying both sides by $I(t)=(100-t)^{-4}$ yields\n$$\\begin{aligned}(100-t)^{-4}\\cdot(\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{100-t}Q)&=(100-t)^{-4}\\cdot 1.44\\\\\n(100-t)^{-4}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+4(100-t)^{-5}Q&=1.44(100-t)^{-4}\\\\\\frac{d}{dt}(Q(100-t)^{-4})&=1.44(100-t)^{-4}.\\end{aligned}$$\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\int\\frac{d}{dt}(Q(100-t)^{-4})\\,dt&=\\int 1.44(100-t)^{-4}\\,dt\\\\Q(100-t)^{-4}&=1.44\\cdot\\frac{1}{3}(100-t)^{-3}+C\\\\Q(t)&=0.48(100-t)+C(100-t)^{4}.\\end{aligned}$$\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\nThe initial condition is $Q(0)=10.$ Thus,\n$$\\begin{aligned}0.48(100-0)+C(100-0)^{4}&=10\\\\\n48+C(100)^{4}&=10\\\\C(100)^{4}&=-38\\\\C&=- \\frac{19}{50000000}.\\end{aligned}$$\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n$$Q(t)=- \\frac{12 t}{25} - \\frac{19 \\left(100 - t\\right)^{4}}{50000000} + 48.$$\nWriting this in the supplied normalized form gives\n$$Q(t)=48(1-\\frac{t}{100})-38(1-\\frac{t}{100})^{4}.$$\nFinally, we have $\\alpha=48,\\beta=-38,$ and\n$$\\alpha-\\beta=48-(-38)=86.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541073 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63c2faca-0edb-5ef8-8326-5900825078b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\cdot {6}^{3/5}}{2}{x}^{11/5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540697} {:answer/id #uuid "73e207b9-91a9-5b1a-8a15-f29313c6b433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{12/5}}{12}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540727} {:answer/id #uuid "da7a61db-d353-5a53-b318-1be353f60fe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{31}^{2/5}}{32}{x}^{7/5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540895} {:answer/id #uuid "ed9e9b62-8e00-5346-a13f-699ec44a7450" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{7}^{2/5}}{2}{x}^{7/5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540927} {:answer/id #uuid "fbfa7707-765d-5937-8a04-fb11b50efc05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{36}^{2/5}}{72}{x}^{12/5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73e207b9-91a9-5b1a-8a15-f29313c6b433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{12/5}}{12}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540727} :answer-field/id #uuid "05367c1f-7c48-5001-bd7f-8cd94f9a1ee7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "31f8bbd7-44e6-58a8-9f8d-def7c80fe8a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-29652" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{7}}?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{7}}={x}^{7/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{7}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{7/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/5+1}}{(\\frac{7}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{12/5}}{\\left(\\frac{12}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{12/5}}{12}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{12/5}}{12}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5{x}^{12/5}}{12}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{12}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{12/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{12}\\cdot \\frac{12}{5}{x}^{12/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{7/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541074 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "141b96db-d2b7-5c1e-ba1e-d4cec0dff378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "136.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540562} {:answer/id #uuid "56903a87-08bb-5210-b21a-15bdc9bdba3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540671} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb90bb5-f5d6-5864-b41d-7da56b21a5e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "136.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540720} {:answer/id #uuid "bba99957-7e8d-5791-b9fd-9c58160a4cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540843} {:answer/id #uuid "c0463d14-2b9e-5fba-b2c4-9802c27cf249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "141b96db-d2b7-5c1e-ba1e-d4cec0dff378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "136.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540562} :answer-field/id #uuid "464a658d-9140-58a9-ae22-53b8f7f33f64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "36a65a87-c8d0-5bbf-adbe-0789f4e08164" :question/math-academy-id "q-336500" :question/problem "A syrup mixing tank at a bottling plant can hold up to $220\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $120\\,\\text{gal}$ of syrup solution with $24\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved sugar. A more concentrated sugar solution with concentration $0.7\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $8\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of sugar in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the tank minus the rate at which sugar leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.7\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of sugar enters the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.7\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.7\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =8.4\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which sugar leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $120\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid; solution enters at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $8\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=120+12t-8t=4(30+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $8\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(8\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{30+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =8.4-\\frac{2Q}{30+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{30+t}\\,Q=8.4.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(30+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|30+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(30+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(30+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(30+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(30+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{30+t}\\,Q) & =(30+t)^{2}\\cdot 8.4 \\\\ (30+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(30+t)Q & =8.4(30+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30+t)^{2}) & =8.4(30+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 8.4(30+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(30+t)^{2} & =8.4\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(30+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =2.8(30+t)+\\frac{C}{(30+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $24\\,\\text{lb}$ of sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=24,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =24 \\\\ 2.8(30+0)+\\frac{C}{(30+0)^{2}} & =24 \\\\ 84+\\frac{C}{(30)^{2}} & =24 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(30)^{2}} & =-60 \\\\ C & =-60(30)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =2.8(30+t)-\\frac{60(30)^{2}}{(30+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =2.8(30+t)-60{\\left(\\frac{30}{30+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $220\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=220$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =220 \\\\ 120+12{t}_{o}-8{t}_{o} & =220 \\\\ 120+4{t}_{o} & =220 \\\\ 4{t}_{o} & =100 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =25\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of sugar in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(25) & =2.8(30+25)-60{\\left(\\frac{30}{30+25}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =154-60{\\left(\\frac{30}{55}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈136.1\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541094 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41a9bbe8-dcee-5623-9122-ba87f8a7d390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "176.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540640} {:answer/id #uuid "52c67f33-11db-51b1-9650-af1cf2900407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "175.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540668} {:answer/id #uuid "a20928a6-6ffa-5ddc-9782-9f840a1c74b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "176.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540798} {:answer/id #uuid "aca174a9-712f-5452-958f-e15b38456d2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "175.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540818} {:answer/id #uuid "d7682d19-2baf-59af-84e2-dbac5b3c49b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "176.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41a9bbe8-dcee-5623-9122-ba87f8a7d390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "176.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540640} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2e476d3-efaf-5ec7-9cea-2c3e7d2d49f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6c55e9d5-5244-5391-84ab-9850b2ba8152" :question/math-academy-id "q-336508" :question/problem "A wastewater treatment tank at a chemical plant can hold up to $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $120\\,\\text{gal}$ of wastewater with $40\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved contaminant. A more concentrated wastewater stream with concentration $1.25\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $8\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.25\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of contaminant enters the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.25\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.25\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =15\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $120\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid; solution enters at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $8\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=120+12t-8t=4(30+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $8\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(8\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{30+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =15-\\frac{2Q}{30+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{30+t}\\,Q=15.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(30+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|30+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(30+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(30+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(30+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(30+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{30+t}\\,Q) & =(30+t)^{2}\\cdot 15 \\\\ (30+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(30+t)Q & =15(30+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30+t)^{2}) & =15(30+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 15(30+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(30+t)^{2} & =15\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(30+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =5(30+t)+\\frac{C}{(30+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $40\\,\\text{lb}$ of contaminant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=40,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =40 \\\\ 5(30+0)+\\frac{C}{(30+0)^{2}} & =40 \\\\ 150+\\frac{C}{(30)^{2}} & =40 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{30}^{2}} & =-110 \\\\ C & =-110(30)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =5(30+t)-\\frac{110(30)^{2}}{(30+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =5(30+t)-110{\\left(\\frac{30}{30+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $180\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=180$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =180 \\\\ 120+12{t}_{o}-8{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ 120+4{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ 4{t}_{o} & =60 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =15\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(15) & =5(30+15)-110{\\left(\\frac{30}{30+15}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =225-110{\\left(\\frac{30}{45}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈176.1\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541108 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c412eada-9cf7-59a5-905e-0f6c0063eb13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t^3}{3}-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c412eada-9cf7-59a5-905e-0f6c0063eb13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t^3}{3}-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540856} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1f591a2-abaf-568b-95d2-b28ddb7d4b0c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "836e24be-b58e-500b-bb05-e9b2030ce5e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-133291" :question/problem "Find $y(t),$ the antiderivative of ${y}^{′}(t).$\n\n$y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C$" :question/worked-solution "We know that ${y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2.$\n\nApplying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {t}^{2}-2\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t-\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t-2\\int {t}^{0}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2+1}}{2+1}\\right)-2t+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-2t+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
